UPDATE "source_value_extractions" SET "root_ref_id" = 'REF-01005', "notes" = 'THE WARRANT IS SCOPED, AND THE SCOPE IS THE FINDING. Table 13 lists four allowable slope bands -- 12.5 percent (1:8), 10.0 percent (1:10), 8.3 percent (1:12) and 6.25 percent (1:16). THE FOOTNOTE MARKER c APPEARS ON THE FIRST TWO AND NOT ON THE LAST TWO. Derive it rather than trusting this sentence: pypdf page index 56 renders the four rows as ''If slope12.5% (1:8)orless steepc.'', ''If slope-10.0% (1:10) or less steepc'', ''If slope*8.3% (1:12) or less.steep'', and ''If. slope.6.25% (1:16) or less steep''. WHY THE READING IS COHERENT RATHER THAN OCR NOISE: Table 12 (page index 55) shows the study tested exactly three slopes, 1:12, 1:16 and 1:20. The two bands carrying footnote c are precisely the two OUTSIDE the tested range, so the report warrants by others exactly what it did not measure itself. WHAT THIS CHANGES: GAP-037 records that Table 13 rests partly on others and treats Templer 1977 and Walter 1971 as the two remaining nodes of the 1:12 chain. That is right about Walter and OVERSTATED about Templer for the anchoring question -- on this reading the named warrant covers the 1:8 and 1:10 rows, the permissive end no determination would rest on, while the 1:12 and 1:16 rows rest on the study''s own data. NO COMPARATOR EDGE: condition_on and derived_from both require a referent this project holds as a row, and Templer 1977 and Walter 1971 are candidates, not rows. The edge becomes writable if either is admitted. STATED AS A READING, NOT AS SETTLED: the markers are single characters on a damaged scan. A cleaner copy of page 57 would confirm or refute it in one look, and until then the scoping claim is held at this confidence and not promoted. [VERBATIM-EXEMPT: OCR-NOT-DECODABLE: scanned 1979 microfiche ED184280.pdf (sha256 5dd866a236fb59788dbff4b4ca80c4da8890274259b0cb6941a83c1c370fb751), bound to REF-01005. quote_in_artefacts cannot decode a PDF text layer. Re-derive with pypdf at page index 56, which holds ''cBased on research of others (Templer, 1977 and-WaJter$, 1971).'' -- the OCR damage is on ''Walter'' and on the footnote marker, which is why claim_text is normalised.] || 2026-09-20 figure_role SET condition -> claim (figure_role was ''condition'' and extraction_relations_integrity refused it, correctly: a condition must QUALIFY something, and this row qualifies no row this project holds. Its referents are Templer 1977 and Walter 1971, which are candidates 115 and 109, not extractions -- condition_on and derived_from both demand a real row, which is why the edge could not be written in the first place. ''claim'' is what the row actually is: REF-01005 ASSERTS, in Table 13 footnote c, that two of its four slope bands rest on others'' research. That is a statement the source makes, not a qualifier on a figure. THE EDGE BECOMES WRITABLE IF EITHER REFERENT IS EVER ADMITTED, and at that point this row can be re-roled to condition with a real condition_on edge. Recorded so the choice reads as a consequence of what the corpus holds rather than as a classification whim.) || 2026-09-20 root_ref_id SET NULL -> REF-01005 (ROOT SUPPLIED, CAUGHT BY A VIEW THIS BATCH FIXED MINUTES EARLIER. Migration 090 gave v_unregistered_roots a rootless arm -- a row asserting a rooted root_type while naming no root by either column -- and the first thing it found was this row, written by me. root_type is committee_assertion with root_ref_id and root_id both NULL, so the typed columns claimed an assertion by nobody. THE ROOT IS REF-01005 ITSELF: Table 13 footnote c is the report own statement about its own recommendation table, which is what committee_assertion means and is how extractions 52-55 are rooted. Setting it self-roots the row the same way. This is the second defect in this batch own extractions that only became visible once the instrument was repaired, which is the argument for repairing instruments rather than rows.)', "updated_at" = '2026-09-20 04:47' WHERE