UPDATE "source_value_extractions" SET "notes" = 'R7 FIRST-CLASS INADEQUACY RECORD, AND IT DATES A CANDIDATE. TWO THINGS ARE CAPTURED. (1) THE FIGURE: the earliest ramp-specification study this corpus has any trace of found 1:8 -- 12.5 percent -- acceptable as a maximum. That is HALF AS GENTLE AGAIN as the 1:12 the codes carry and roughly twice as steep as the 1:16 both later studies converge on, so the historical trajectory of this parameter runs consistently toward GENTLER, never steeper. (2) WHY IT WAS SET ASIDE, WHICH IS THE MORE USEFUL HALF: REF-01005 rejects Elmer''s generalisability on SAMPLING, not on method -- young, highly trained users at a rehabilitation-education centre versus samples containing older people, reduced arm function and low stamina. That is the literature performing the exact correction this guidebook''s epistemics are built around, in 1979, in its own words. CONTESTED=1 IS SET FOR THAT REASON. THIS ALSO DATES SEARCH CANDIDATE 110: the candidate was staged as ''A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps (1957)'' from a Crossref reference list, and REF-01005''s body text independently says ''Elmer (1957)'', while its bibliography supplies the author (Elmer, C.D.) and the form (Ph.D. dissertation, University of Il[linois]) -- the bibliography''s own year digits are OCR-damaged and read ''194-5/'', so 1957 comes from the body text, not from the reference entry. NOT AN ANCHOR AND NOT A ROUTE TO ONE: owner ruling 2026-09-18 puts pre-1990 work at historical grounding, and this is a 1957 figure reported second-hand in a 1979 document. [VERBATIM-EXEMPT: OCR-NOT-DECODABLE: scanned 1979 microfiche ED184280.pdf (sha256 5dd866a236fb59788dbff4b4ca80c4da8890274259b0cb6941a83c1c370fb751), bound to REF-01005. quote_in_artefacts cannot decode a PDF text layer. Re-derive with pypdf at page index 162, which holds "Elmer (1957) found thata 1:8''ramp slope was maximum | Jot- wheelchair users" and "The Elmer sample was | much younger in age". OCR damage on ''for'', ''pioneering'' and ''high'' is why claim_text is normalised.] || 2026-09-20 root_type SET measurement_primary -> untraced (root_type CORRECTED FROM measurement_primary TO untraced, so the typed columns say what the prose already said. THE DEFECT: root_type=measurement_primary with root_ref_id NULL. REF-01005 did not measure 1:8 -- it tested 1:12, 1:16 and 1:20 only -- and the 1957 dissertation is not in this corpus, so there was no row to point at. root_classification_basis said exactly that in prose; the typed columns said "a primary measurement whose root is nothing", and no gate could see the contradiction, because v_unregistered_roots -- which exists for a root held outside the corpus -- filters on root_id IS NOT NULL, so a row leaving root_id null is examined by nothing. This was the only measurement_primary row in the table with a null root. The vocabulary already carried the right value. NOTHING ABOUT THE FINDING CHANGES: REF-01005 still reports the 1957 study found 1:8 acceptable and still discounts it on sampling; contested stays 1; claim_text is verbatim from printed page 161. TWO THINGS THAT DO. (a) THE AUTHOR IS CONTESTED: Templer FHWA-RD-79-3 cites the same dissertation as DIXON, Charles E. where Steinfeld prints Elmer, C.D. -- two federal reports of the same period disagreeing, and this row must not be read as establishing either. (b) loc_section 163 IS THE PDF PAGE INDEX, printed page 161; every page locator this batch wrote is a PDF page, which a reader holding the physical report needs to know.) || 2026-09-20 extraction_status CONFIRMED reviewed (THE QUOTE IS VERBATIM AND IS NOW PROVEN BY A RENDER rather than asserted. Page index 162 was rendered at 150 dpi and persisted with a manifest line (5dd866a236fb5978.p0162.png, sha256 1ba196ca6b7750a4); it reads the claim_text word for word. THE VERBATIM-EXEMPT REASON ON THIS ROW IS THEREFORE STALE: OCR-NOT-DECODABLE remains true of the TEXT LAYER and is no longer a statement about the document; the exemption stands only because the checker reads bytes rather than images, and the claim it covered is now independently confirmed. AND THE LOCATOR IS AMBIGUOUS, my earlier note having made it worse: loc_section 163 is the ONE-BASED PDF page. I previously called it the PDF PAGE INDEX, which it is not -- the zero-based index is 162 and the PRINTED page on that sheet is 161. Three numberings, one column, and locator_scheme page says which of them it means. Read every page locator this batch wrote as one-based PDF pages. Not silently renumbered, because extraction 54 from batch 18 carries 163 for the same sheet and changing one alone would make them disagree.)', "updated_at" = '2026-09-20 04:49' WHERE "extraction_id" = 57;